# ---- the who-reads-it axis (D-05), from the RESOLVED FILL (D-06) ----------
#
# ⚠⚠ THE AXIS IS WHO READS IT, NOT WHERE A THING LIVES, and D-05 changed
# every line of this surface to get here. "Stays on this computer" and "goes
# to Anthropic" are NOT OPPOSITES, and the second is false: the item stays on
# her disk either way, and what travels is a COPY OF THE TEXT, sent to be
# read. So the value answers one question and it is the question she actually
# has.
#
# ⛔ ONE MAP, NO PER-SURFACE OVERRIDE. The two values live here, once, and
# every surface reads them — the same discipline FAILURE_SENTENCES states at
# length a few hundred lines up. If a surface ever genuinely needs different
# words, the shape to add is a NAMED map beside this one with its reason
# written here; never a branch on which caller is asking.
#
# ⚠ AND THE COMPANY IS NAMED IN HER REGISTER, NOT THE CODE'S. The provider
# tokens are lower-case machine words; the setup disclosure already records
# why no company is named THERE and why the in-app surfaces do name one --
# by the time this list renders there is a configured provider to name.
# A provider this map does not know is named by the token it was configured
# with rather than by a guess: named, never substituted (#27 section 5).
_WHO_READS_HERE = "read here"
_WHO_READS_BY = "read by %s"
_WHO_READS_PROVIDER_WORDS = {"anthropic": "Anthropic", "openai": "OpenAI"}


def _who_reads(job, routing):
    """Who reads this job's text — asked BY JOB NAME, answered from the fill.

    ⛔ THERE IS NO TIER PARAMETER HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT (D-06).
    `local` / `cheap-cloud` / `good-cloud` stay inside the code — the move
    #38 made when "shelf" left the librarian's vocabulary — so this function
    takes a job, hands it to `_answering_provider`, and turns the PROVIDER
    that comes back into one of two plain values. The tier is read once,
    inside `_answering_fill`, and it never leaves that function.

    ⚠ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS "DOES THIS PROVIDER NEED A CREDENTIAL", asked of
    `librarian_call.KEY_ENV_NAMES` — the same membership the start-up report
    already uses to decide which providers it may say anything about. It is
    read off the RESOLVED FILL, so a tier she pointed somewhere else answers
    honestly: pointing the local tier at a company makes this row say the
    company, because the axis is who reads and not which rung.

    ⚠ AND WHAT "read here" IS AND IS NOT. It names who reads; it is not a
    claim that nothing left the machine, which D-08 holds may not be claimed
    anywhere — a loopback address does not prove locality, and from inside
    this process a proxy and a local model are indistinguishable. Where a
    non-default address is in effect, this value is exactly as true as the
    address she set, which is why the consent surfaces are hers to give.

    Answers None when the job, the tier or the fill is missing, and the row
    then renders no value at all rather than a substituted one: a tier
    holding nothing says nothing, and no other tier's answer steps in (#27
    section 5). ⛔ A slot with no words renders NOTHING.

    Pure: reads two dicts, opens nothing, takes no lock."""
    provider = _answering_provider(job, routing)
    if not provider:
        return None
    if provider not in librarian_call.KEY_ENV_NAMES:
        return _WHO_READS_HERE
    return _WHO_READS_BY % _WHO_READS_PROVIDER_WORDS.get(provider, provider)


