# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---- what a call cost, in the provider's OWN counts (26.93-06, D-02) ------
#
# ⛔⛔ NO DOLLAR FIGURE IS COMPUTED HERE, OR ANYWHERE ELSE IN THIS FILE, AND NO
# PRICE TABLE ENTERS THIS REPO. The seam stopped returning money because it has
# no business holding a rate table; adding one here would move the same problem
# one file up and hand it a way to go stale silently. What is recorded is what
# the provider itself reported — token counts, per provider, plus how many
# calls they came from.
#
# HOW SPEND IS MEASURED AND SHOWN IS MAP TICKET #34's, open and downstream.
# This plan owes #34 exactly one thing and pays it: the data, on the run record
# and on the job snapshot, waiting for whatever #34 decides. Until then the
# room says nothing about money at all — a room that stops claiming a number it
# no longer has is honest, and a fabricated one is not.
#
# The three providers report the same two facts under different names, so the
# names are listed rather than branched on; the first one present wins.
_USAGE_INPUT_NAMES = ("input_tokens", "prompt_tokens", "prompt_eval_count")
_USAGE_OUTPUT_NAMES = ("output_tokens", "completion_tokens", "eval_count")


def accumulate_usage(bucket, usage):
    """Fold ONE call's reported counts into a running per-provider total.

    `bucket` is {provider: {calls, input_tokens, output_tokens}} — bounded by
    the number of providers, never by the number of calls, so a 300-batch
    import's record stays three small rows rather than three hundred.

    Fail-open by construction: an absent, off-shape or partial `usage` adds
    nothing and raises nothing. A provider that reports no counts still counts
    its call, because "we asked three hundred times" is true and useful even
    when the numbers are missing. Returns the same bucket for convenience."""
    if not isinstance(bucket, dict) or not isinstance(usage, dict):
        return bucket
    provider = usage.get("provider") or "unknown"
    counts = usage.get("counts")
    counts = counts if isinstance(counts, dict) else usage
    row = bucket.setdefault(str(provider), {"calls": 0, "input_tokens": 0,
                                            "output_tokens": 0})
    row["calls"] += 1
    for names, field in ((_USAGE_INPUT_NAMES, "input_tokens"),
                         (_USAGE_OUTPUT_NAMES, "output_tokens")):
        for name in names:
            value = counts.get(name)
            if isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool):
                row[field] += value
                break
    return bucket


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---- 26.99-03 (D-01, D-02, SRM-13): the record, written AROUND the seam ----
#
# ⚠ WHY THE WRAPPER LIVES IN THIS FILE AND NOT AT THE SEAM. `librarian_call`
# is deliberately store-free — `resolve_routing`'s own docstring is the
# claim (*"writes nothing, takes no lock"*), the module has no library root
# and no directory of its own, and SRM-13 rests on exactly one function
# carrying store bytes to a model while being provably a pure transport.
# Teaching that function to open a file would end the property, and the
# alternative — a fourth parameter — widens a signature `test_call_seam.py`
# pins. So the record is written AROUND the one call, by a wrapper in the
# file that already owns every file this room writes.
#
# ⛔ AND THE SIX `accumulate_usage` SITES ARE NOT THE PERSISTENCE POINT.
# This is the choice the phase exists to refuse, so it is refused in
# writing rather than by omission. They are a FOLD: they collapse many
# calls into one row per provider, so they can answer *what did it cost*
# and structurally cannot answer *what ran*. They cannot see a call that
# reported nothing — a local answer, a refusal, a failure — and those are
# precisely the lines D-01 asks for. And they are SIX, which is six places
# to keep one rule true and five ways to drift.
#
# ⚠ WHAT WOULD OVERTURN THIS: a seam that grew a library root of its own,
# or a ruling that the record answers only what was spent. Neither holds
# today.
#
# ✅ COVERAGE IS NO LONGER PARTIAL, AND THE NOTE THAT SAID SO IS REPLACED
# RATHER THAN LEFT TO ROT. 26.99-03 wired ONE of the nine measured seam
# call sites — the import pre-sort — and said out loud that the
# other eight were owed. 26.99-04 routed all eight through this wrapper, so
# the seam is now reached from exactly one place in this file and a call
# with no line is structurally impossible rather than conventionally
# avoided. `SEAM_CALL_SITES` below is what keeps it that way.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ⚠ THE MEASURED NUMBER OF PLACES IN THIS FILE THAT REACH THE SEAM, AND IT
# IS ONE: `record_call`'s own call, below. Every production caller names its
# job at the wrapper instead, so every call the room makes leaves a line.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT COUNTS, AND WHY IT IS A COUNT. `tests/test_spend_record.py`
# reads this file with comments and string literals blanked, counts the
# references to the seam by name, and asserts the total against this
# constant — over the syntax tree as well, so the one
# permitted site is exempted by the FUNCTION IT SITS IN and never by its
# shape. A count rather than a review because the failure it guards is
# silent: a caller added around the wrapper does not break anything, it
# just stops appearing in the file the room offers as evidence that
# nothing was hidden. ⚠ A record that silently misses calls is worse than
# no record, because it is offered as evidence.
#
# ⛔ IT IS NOT A NUMBER TO RAISE WHEN A NEW CALLER APPEARS. Raising it is
# exactly how the record goes quietly partial, and the instrument pins the
# same value independently so that raising one is not enough. A caller that
# genuinely needs the seam directly is an owner-level decision about what
# the privacy record may omit, not a constant edit.
#
# ⚠ WHAT WOULD OVERTURN IT: a job whose call cannot carry a line at all
# (none exists — the wrapper takes the seam's own three arguments and
# returns the seam's own answer unchanged), or a caller OUTSIDE `server.py`.
# ⛔ THE SECOND IS A REAL LIMIT AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN IMPLIED:
# this gate reads one file. Nothing outside it reaches the seam today —
# measured — and `tests/test_no_push.cjs` is what keeps store bytes
# travelling through the one function, but a new module calling the seam
# would be invisible here.
SEAM_CALL_SITES = 1

# Its own small lock, and it stays small: it serializes the read-modify-
# write of one file and nothing else. ⚠ Never WRITE_LOCK (the store's) and
# never LIBRARIAN_LOCK (which must stay cheap) — `_BLESSINGS_LOCK`'s rule,
# one concern over: nothing held under this lock takes another.
_CALL_RECORD_LOCK = threading.Lock()

_CALL_RECORD_LIMIT_MSG = "limit must be a whole number between 1 and 500."


def _reported_tokens(usage, names):
    """One provider-reported count, or 0 when it reported none.

    ⚠ ZERO IS A REAL ANSWER HERE RATHER THAN A MISSING ONE, and that is
    D-01's whole point: a local answer and a refused call both genuinely
    reported nothing, and a line that omitted them would answer what was
    spent rather than what ran. The three providers report the same two
    facts under different names, so the names are read from the SAME lists
    `accumulate_usage` reads — never a second copy. Pure."""
    counts = usage.get("counts")
    counts = counts if isinstance(counts, dict) else usage
    for name in names:
        value = counts.get(name)
        if isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool):
            return value
    return 0


def _record_word(value):
    """A record field as a plain string, or None. The file is hers to edit,
    so the reader hands back a word or nothing — never whatever it found."""
    return value if isinstance(value, str) else None


def _record_int(value):
    """A record field as a whole number, or None. ⚠ `bool` is tested FIRST:
    `True` is an `int` in Python, so a hand-edited `true` would otherwise
    read back as 1."""
    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int):
        return None
    return value


def record_call(job, payload_text, routing):
    """`librarian_call.call_librarian`, with one line written down.

    ⚠ THE SIGNATURE IS THE SEAM'S, EXACTLY, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING TWICE
    OVER. A caller names a job, hands over the text, and receives the
    seam's own answer back UNCHANGED — so wiring a call site is a one-word
    edit and nothing downstream learns a new shape; and 26.99-04's static
    gate can then tell a wired caller from a bypassing one by the name it
    typed.

    SIX FIELDS AND SIX ONLY (D-01): when, job, provider, model, tokens in,
    tokens out. ⛔ No item id, no title, no path (law 5, L-06) — a record
    that named her things would put never-list material in the one place it
    would be most damaging. ⛔ Nothing in a currency, no rate and no derived
    total, at any depth.

    ⚠ WHO ANSWERED COMES FROM THE ROUTING, NOT FROM THE ANSWER, through
    `_answering_fill`'s indirection — so the tier name never enters the
    record (D-06), and a call that never reached anybody still records who
    would have taken it.

    ⚠ A LINE IS WRITTEN WHATEVER HAPPENED — a refusal, a failure, and a
    local answer reporting no counts all get one, with zeroes. The file
    answers what RAN; the folds above already answer the other question.

    ⚠ THE RECORD NEVER FAILS THE CALL, and the trade is stated rather than
    hidden: a disk that cannot be written costs her the LINE, not the run
    she was in the middle of. The cost of that choice is real — a record
    can be quietly incomplete — and closing it wants a surface that can say
    so, which is not this plan's."""
    started_ms = int(time.time() * 1000)
    result = librarian_call.call_librarian(job, payload_text, routing)
    fill = _answering_fill(job, routing)
    usage = result.get("usage") if isinstance(result, dict) else None
    usage = usage if isinstance(usage, dict) else {}
    line = {"at": started_ms,
            "job": str(job),
            "provider": fill[0] if fill else None,
            "model": fill[1] if fill else None,
            "input_tokens": _reported_tokens(usage, _USAGE_INPUT_NAMES),
            "output_tokens": _reported_tokens(usage, _USAGE_OUTPUT_NAMES)}
    # ⛔⛔ F10 TRIED TO WRITE A `usd` KEY HERE ON 2026-08-23 AND IT WAS WRONG.
    # `tests/test_spend_record.py` caught it, and the reason is D-01/D-02 and
    # not a style rule: THIS FILE IS A PRIVACY LEDGER WEARING A LEDGER'S
    # CLOTHES. Read one way it is a bill; read the other it is everything this
    # app has ever sent and to whom, and it is the only thing in the room that
    # answers *has my privacy been kept* with evidence rather than a promise.
    # ⛔ THE SIX FIELDS ARE A CEILING, NOT A STARTING POINT — a seventh key
    # fails that suite on purpose — and money is banned outright, because a
    # record that reads as a bill stops being read as a privacy record.
    # ⛔ DO NOT ADD A COST FIELD HERE. `call_usd` exists (F10) and is
    # deliberately UNCALLED; see its own block for where its number belongs.
    try:
        with _CALL_RECORD_LOCK:
            study_lib.merge_call_record(
                study_lib.call_record_path(), [line])
    except OSError:
        pass
    return result


# ⚠ A CACHED LOOK-UP FOR AN INSTALLED PROGRAM USED TO SIT HERE, WITH THE
# THREE SMALL FUNCTIONS THAT SERVED IT AND THE ONE THAT GUESSED HOW THE ROOM
# WAS AUTHENTICATED (26.93-07). They existed only to ask a binary whether it
# was there and whether it was new enough. Nothing behind the librarian is a
# binary any more, so all four questions stopped existing at once; the
# availability answer below is asked of the routing object instead, and the
# one place in this file that still wants a program on PATH now asks for it
# itself, at its own point of use. Their cache and its lock went with them —
# state kept warm for readers that no longer exist is a trap, not a saving.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---- the front door: who is answering, asked of ROUTING (26.93-08) --------
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS REPLACED, AND WHY THE REPLACEMENT WAS BUILT BEFORE THE OLD THING
# WAS REMOVED. Availability used to be a PATH look-up for an installed program
# plus a version read off it, cached for the life of the process. 26.93-08 built
# this answer beside that one; 26.93-07 then took the old family away. Deleting
# first would have left the room's one availability answer broken for a whole
# wave — building beside, then removing, is the order the seam itself was built
# in, and it is why this section reads as a replacement rather than a repair.
#
# ⚠ THE THREE CLI-ERA ANSWERS BECAME ONE ROUTING-SHAPED QUESTION. "Not set up",
# "too old" and "ready" were three facts about a binary. There is no binary to
# version-check any more, so the question is: does this tier have a fill, does
# its provider have something to authenticate with, and — when the fill is her
# own machine — is the model there. The two sentences that answered the two old
# questions were deleted with them; keeping copy nobody can reach is how a room
# ends up able to say something that is no longer true.
#
# ⚠ THE `auth` FIELD IS A CONSTANT, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION RATHER THAN A
# TIDY-UP. It used to be a guess: a key-shaped answer when a key name was in the
# environment, and a subscription-shaped one otherwise. `_presort_worker` still
# branches on it, and the subscription arm answers a failed call with
# LIBRARIAN_WINDOW_MSG — a sentence about a plan's usage window being full.
# There is no subscription behind this seam any more: every cloud rung
# authenticates with a key and the local rung authenticates with nothing. Under
# the old guess that arm would answer an Ollama that is not running with a
# sentence about a plan she may not even have, which is precisely the
# mislabelling D-06 and D-07 exist to prevent — so the token is pinned to the
# one branch that produces a true sentence, and the arm below it is unreachable
# by construction. The field keeps its name and its shape so no surface has to
# change to receive it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The one auth token the rebuilt seam can honestly report (see just above).
LIBRARIAN_AUTH_TOKEN = "api-key"

# Which trouble the room explains first when SEVERAL fills are unusable at once.
# A rejected credential leads because it is the only one of the four that is
# about HER: a company looked at what she gave it and refused it, and one
# command puts a fresh one in. `no_key` follows for the same reason. The local
# rung's two come last — they are about a program that is not running on this
# machine, and their own sentences already carry the exact command that starts
# it. ⚠ An explicit literal, never a derivation over the tables above: an order
# that changes because something else changed shape is an order nobody edited.
_UNAVAILABLE_ORDER = ("bad_key", "no_key", "model_not_pulled",
                      "ollama_not_running")

# How a provenance token reads in the one line a person actually sees. These are
# machine tokens everywhere else and stay machine tokens; this is the only place
# they become words. `env_rejected` gets a phrase of its own because fail-closed
# is correct but INVISIBLE is not — a shell value the gate refused must not look
# identical to a plain default.
_PROVENANCE_WORDS = {
    librarian_call.SOURCE_STORED: "your setting",
    librarian_call.SOURCE_ENV: "the shell",
    librarian_call.SOURCE_ENV_REJECTED:
        "the shell, refused — the shipped default is in use",
    librarian_call.SOURCE_DEFAULT: "the shipped default",
}

# The three tiers in plain words. A tier name is a machine token and stays one.
_TIER_WORDS = {
    "local": "your own machine",
    "cheap-cloud": "everyday jobs",
    "good-cloud": "the longer writing",
}


def _ollama_base(routing):
    """Where her own machine answers, for this run. Pure."""
    bases = getattr(routing, "bases", None) or {}
    return bases.get("ollama") or librarian_call.DEFAULT_BASES["ollama"]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---- 26.99-09: the address she has to agree to (D-09..D-12, D-15, D-19) ---
#
# ⛔⛔ D-10, AND IT IS STRONGER THAN "THE CHECK IS EXEMPT": no call of any kind
# reaches a non-default destination before consent. `check_key` is simply the
# first call that would have happened, and the ruling makes FOLLOWING the
# override safe rather than exempting it.
#
# ⛔ AND D-19 READS D-10 WIDE, BECAUSE THE RULING WAS ALREADY BEING BROKEN BY A
# PATH NOBODY HAD NAMED. `librarian_call.resolve_routing`'s settings `bases`
# loop has been accepting an override FOR ALL THREE PROVIDERS — its own comment
# concedes that nobody should build on it — and `startup_librarian_check`
# probes the local base on EVERY ROOM OPEN. So a hand-edited settings file has
# been pointing the room's FIRST network call of every session at an arbitrary
# host, with nothing asked. No credential rides that one, so it is not a key
# leak; D-10's words are "no call of ANY kind", and this is a call of a kind.
# ⛔ The narrow reading was refused in writing — it would leave a sentence in
# the decision record that this code does not honour, which is the exact
# failure map #62 exists to fix. The finding is routed to #12.
#
# ⚠⚠ SO THE GATE SITS ON THE START-UP PATH EVERY USER HITS, AND IT IS BUILT TO
# BE INERT. `librarian_call.base_is_shipped_default` is pure and answers FIRST,
# so a room that redirected nothing does not open her settings file on account
# of this, does not probe differently, does not prompt, and gains no new way to
# fail. ✅ Law 6 survives exactly there: the prompt can only ever appear to
# somebody who set the address themselves.
#
# ⛔ AND NOTHING HERE CLAIMS LOCALITY (D-08). A redirected address is very
# often a loopback one and a loopback address does not prove locality —
# LiteLLM on `localhost:4000` forwards to the cloud and is indistinguishable
# from Ollama on `localhost:11434` from inside this file. Her sentences below
# say where the key GOES; not one of them says anything stays.

# ⚠ HER SENTENCE, BYTE-FOR-BYTE from 26.99-COPY.md §S-08 + §S-09. ⛔ Not
# recapitalised, not repunctuated, not tidied, not wrapped. D-14: front-facing
# wording is the owner's, taken as one pass.
#
# ⚠⚠ TWO SLOTS, ONE SENTENCE, ONE CONSTANT — her explicit ruling was "the same
# wording in both", and here that is load-bearing rather than tidy: D-11 says
# the two surfaces must AGREE, and two literals are two chances to disagree.
# The terminal prints this constant; the room is SENT this constant over the
# status route and holds no copy of its own. ⛔ A second literal at either site
# is forbidden by §S-08's rule 3, and a case asserts the count is exactly one.
#
# ⚠ One safety flag was raised to her during the copy pass and she closed it:
# her first draft omitted the key entirely, so a reader could have said yes
# believing the question was only about which service answers. "Your key will
# be sent there." is the sentence she approved to close that hole. ⛔ It is not
# an agent's addition and it is not removable as redundant.
BASE_CONSENT_ASK = "Looks like you've pointed this to somewhere that is not a typical commercial AI service provider. Your key will be sent there. Press yes if that's your preference."  # noqa: E501

# ⚠ HER SENTENCE, BYTE-FOR-BYTE from 26.99-COPY.md §S-10, and ONE constant for
# the same reason as the question above it.
#
# ⚠ D-15: declining leaves the librarian off FOR THAT VISIT and the room still
# opens — the base app has never required a key, so everything that is not the
# librarian still works. ⛔ Her first draft framed the decline as a breakage and
# asked for troubleshooting steps; that was put back to her, because nothing is
# broken and it lasts one visit. ⛔ NO TROUBLESHOOTING STEPS ARE WRITTEN
# ANYWHERE — there is nothing to troubleshoot.
BASE_CONSENT_DECLINED_MSG = "You said no, so the librarian sits out this visit. The room will remain open but all of the services related to the librarian will be unavailable until next time."  # noqa: E501

# ⚠ HER SENTENCE, BYTE-FOR-BYTE from 26.99-COPY.md §S-11.
#
# ⚠ D-12: WARN, DO NOT BLOCK. The structural alternative — run the sorting
# passes and refuse the reflection pass — was rejected because it hard-codes
# one judgement about other people's models into a project meant to be forked.
# So this stands BESIDE the consent surfaces and stops nothing. ⚠ It is a fact
# about the MODEL, not about the consent, which is why it keeps standing after
# she has agreed: what she agreed to was the destination, not the quality.
#
# ⚠ Her first draft named only the reflection; the silent-truncation hazard was
# flagged to her as missing (D-12 names it as the second hazard) and she
# approved its addition.
BASE_MODEL_QUALITY_MSG = "Looks like you are using a non-commercial AI service provider, just for the heads up, 7B local model can cause the quality of reflection downgrades and the answer can stop partway without telling you."  # noqa: E501


def base_overrides(routing):
    """[(provider, address)] for every address in effect that this app does not
    ship — the WIDE reading, so her own machine's address is in it too (D-19).

    ⚠ PURE, AND THAT IS THE PROPERTY THE START-UP PATH DEPENDS ON. It reads the
    frozen routing object and compares against the shipped constants; it opens
    no file and asks nobody. A room that redirected nothing gets an empty list
    for the price of three string comparisons, which is why putting a gate on
    the front door costs it nothing."""
    bases = getattr(routing, "bases", None) or {}
    return [(provider, bases[provider]) for provider in sorted(bases)
            if not librarian_call.base_is_shipped_default(bases[provider],
                                                          provider)]


def base_consent_gap(routing):
    """(provider, address, state) for the FIRST address in effect that was not
    agreed to, or None when every address in effect is one she agreed to or one
    this app ships.

    ⚠ THE OFF IS DERIVED FROM THE ABSENCE OF CONSENT, NEVER FROM THE PRESENCE
    OF A DECLINE — 26.99-08's shape, and one rule covering *never asked* and
    *declined this visit* together. `state` is carried alongside because a
    SURFACE also needs to know whether to ASK, which is a different question
    from whether to STOP: `unasked` asks, `declined` does not ask again this
    visit, and both are OFF.

    ⚠⚠ ONE AT A TIME, AND THE LIMITATION IS WRITTEN HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE
    DISCOVERED. The record holds ONE address (26.99-08's L-03: the value agreed
    to, never a flag), so two DIFFERENT redirected hosts cannot both be agreed
    to at once — agreeing to the second replaces the first. ✅ The direction
    that matters is that this is SAFE rather than convenient: the address that
    is not agreed to stays OFF and nothing reaches it, and her per-visit
    decline is the exit from a surface that would otherwise alternate between
    two questions. ⛔ Routed to #12. What would close it is the record holding a
    SET of addresses, which is `librarian_call`'s to change and not this
    plan's — and a case pins the limitation so it cannot be met as a surprise.

    ⚠ Fail-closed in both directions: an address that normalises to nothing is
    not an address anybody agreed to, and `base_needs_consent` says so."""
    for provider, base in base_overrides(routing):
        if librarian_call.base_needs_consent(base, provider):
            return provider, base, librarian_call.base_consent_state(base)
    return None


def base_agreed(provider, routing):
    """Whether the address THIS provider would be reached at is one she agreed
    to, or one this app ships.

    ⚠ THE ONE PREDICATE BOTH `--setup`'S DISCLOSURE AND `--setup`'S CALL READ.
    Two spellings would let the sentence promise a request the gate then
    refuses, and a setup that says "checking it now" and checks nothing is a
    setup that has lied about a network call — which is the one category of
    sentence this file's own docstring is written to prevent."""
    base = (getattr(routing, "bases", None) or {}).get(provider)
    return not librarian_call.base_needs_consent(base, provider)


def base_consent_why(state):
    """The plain sentence for a librarian that is off because an address in
    effect was not agreed to. ⚠ BOTH ARE HERS, and each is ONE literal.

    A DECLINE gets §S-10, which is written in the past tense because she did
    say no. ANYTHING ELSE — chiefly *never asked* — gets the question itself,
    because on an address nobody has put the question to her about, the honest
    answer to "why is the librarian unavailable" is the question that is
    waiting, and §S-10 there would tell her she said something she never
    said."""
    if state == librarian_call.CONSENT_STATE_DECLINED:
        return BASE_CONSENT_DECLINED_MSG
    return BASE_CONSENT_ASK


def base_consent_block():
    """The room's half of the consent, as fields on the SHIPPED status route.

    ⛔ NOT A WIDENING OF `librarian_available` (T-26.99-43). Three consumers
    read that object's five keys, and widening it for one surface is exactly
    how the `auth`-field inversion defect happened. These fields ride
    `handle_librarian_status` beside `photo_reading_ok`, which is the shape
    that route already established for a fact that is not an availability.

    ⚠ RESOLVED FRESH ON EVERY CALL, NEVER CACHED, on the resolve-time
    discipline `resolve_librarian_routing` states one screen down: the terminal
    may have written the record since this page was painted, and D-11's
    cross-surface re-ask IS that read.

    ⚠ THE QUALITY WARNING IS NOT GATED ON THE CONSENT (D-12) — see
    `BASE_MODEL_QUALITY_MSG`. It stands while a non-default address is in
    effect, including after she has agreed, and it blocks nothing.

    ⛔ NO CREDENTIAL AT ANY DEPTH. Every value here is an address or one of her
    own sentences; nothing in this function reads the keys file.

    Fail-open, like every other read of a file that is hers to edit: an
    unreadable settings file answers "nothing to say" rather than raising into
    a handler."""
    quiet = {"base_consent_needed": False, "base_consent_address": None,
             "base_consent_ask": None, "base_consent_quality": None}
    try:
        routing = resolve_librarian_routing()
        overrides = base_overrides(routing)
        gap = base_consent_gap(routing)
    except Exception:
        return quiet
    if not overrides:
        # ⛔ A ROOM THAT REDIRECTED NOTHING GETS THE SHIPPED ANSWER, and the
        # room renders nothing at all from it. Law 6.
        return quiet
    block = dict(quiet)
    block["base_consent_quality"] = BASE_MODEL_QUALITY_MSG
    if gap is None:
        return block
    _provider, base, state = gap
    block["base_consent_needed"] = True
    block["base_consent_address"] = base
    # ⛔ A SLOT WITH NO QUESTION RENDERS NOTHING. Within one visit a decline
    # suppresses the second ask (D-15), so the room keeps the warning and her
    # §S-10 sentence — which arrives through `librarian_available`'s `why` —
    # and does not put the same question to her twice.
    if state == librarian_call.CONSENT_STATE_UNASKED:
        block["base_consent_ask"] = BASE_CONSENT_ASK
    return block


def update_status_block(stamp, include_result=False):
    """The room's half of the daily-check consent, as fields on the SHIPPED
    status route (26.9997 D-01..D-03, D-09). Fields, not a surface.

    Resolved fresh on every call, never cached: the Manage switch, the
    terminal and the toolbar question all write one record. A dev tree
    (no stamp) gets `update_consent_state: None` and no question at all,
    because a dev tree never checks and never offers (D-09). The question
    travels only while the state is unasked AND the tree is stamped; an
    empty slot renders nothing on the client. `update_install_ready` is
    True exactly when the tap would not be refused for state reasons:
    behind AND consented (D-10).

    `include_result` is True ONLY on the status route: the helper's one
    result note is read once and then gone (D-17), and the one read
    belongs to the next status call, never to a consent answer's block.

    Fail-open: an unreadable settings file answers the quiet shape rather
    than raising into a handler. No credential at any depth."""
    quiet = {"update_consent_state": None,
             "update_install_ready": False}
    if not stamp:
        return quiet
    try:
        state = study_lib.update_consent_state()
    except Exception:
        return quiet
    block = dict(quiet)
    block["update_consent_state"] = state
    block["update_consent_host"] = study_lib.UPDATE_SOURCE_HOST
    if state == study_lib.UPDATE_CONSENT_STATE_UNASKED:
        block["update_consent_ask"] = OWNER_COPY_UPDATE_CONSENT_ASK
    try:
        behind = study_lib.compute_show_update_prompt(
            stamp, study_lib.read_latest_release_date())
    except Exception:
        behind = False
    block["update_install_ready"] = bool(
        behind and state == study_lib.UPDATE_CONSENT_STATE_CONSENTED)
    if include_result:
        try:
            result = study_lib.read_update_result_once()
        except Exception:
            result = None
        if result is not None:
            block["update_result"] = result
    return block


def record_update_consent_answer(host, answer):
    """Record her answer to the daily-check question and return the block.
    The route's whole body, kept out of the handler so a suite can drive it
    without a socket.

    Fail-closed on the host: the answer names the host the page SHOWED, and
    it is recorded only when that is the one host this room would ever ask
    (`study_lib.UPDATE_SOURCE_HOST`). Fail-closed on the answer too: only
    the yes token grants; anything else is a durable no. Nothing secret is
    read, written or echoed."""
    try:
        stamp = study_lib.read_release_stamp(REPO_ROOT)
    except Exception:
        stamp = None
    if host != study_lib.UPDATE_SOURCE_HOST:
        return update_status_block(stamp)
    try:
        study_lib.record_update_consent(
            OWNER_COPY_UPDATE_CONSENT_YES
            if answer == OWNER_COPY_UPDATE_CONSENT_YES else "no")
    except Exception:
        pass
    return update_status_block(stamp)


# The one running StudyServer, set in main(): the Windows hand-off branch
# must be able to shut the server down after starting the detached helper.
SERVER_INSTANCE = None

# The daily release check runs from the status route on a short-lived
# daemon thread joined with this bounded wait. urlopen's timeout bounds
# connect and read but NOT getaddrinfo, and the first status answer of the
# day gates the initial room paint: on a black-holed resolver (captive
# portal, half-down Wi-Fi) a synchronous check could stall that paint for
# the OS resolver timeout. On a healthy network the thread finishes inside
# the wait and the answer still lands in the SAME status read; past the
# wait the room paints now and the result lands on a later poll.
UPDATE_CHECK_STATUS_WAIT_S = 2.0

# tools/update_room.py, imported by path and cached per path. REPO_ROOT is
# patchable (suites point it at a temp tree), so the cache key is the path.
_UPDATE_ROOM_MODULES = {}


def _update_room_module():
    """The helper beside this server, imported by path from the live app
    folder so the in-process unpack/verify and the exec argv agree on ONE
    file (D-13)."""
    import importlib.util
    path = str(REPO_ROOT / "tools" / "update_room.py")
    mod = _UPDATE_ROOM_MODULES.get(path)
    if mod is not None:
        return mod
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(
        "_studyroom_update_helper_%d" % len(_UPDATE_ROOM_MODULES), path)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    _UPDATE_ROOM_MODULES[path] = mod
    return mod


def update_job_running():
    """True while any job whose worker could outlive the swap is running.

    The five job dicts the install tap refuses on (RESEARCH Pitfall 7):
    librarian, ask, import, collect export, vision read. Their workers are
    daemon threads INSIDE this process, and the exec skips exit handlers,
    so an exec while one runs kills it mid-write. Checked once before the
    download, AGAIN after it (the download may block minutes, and a job
    started in another tab during that window must still refuse the swap),
    and once more at the hand-off itself."""
    with LIBRARIAN_LOCK:
        states = [LIBRARIAN_JOB.get("state"), ASK_JOB.get("state")]
    with JOB_LOCK:
        states += [IMPORT_JOB.get("state"), EXPORT_JOB.get("state"),
                   VISION_JOB.get("state")]
        if VISION_JOB.get("state") == "pending":
            states.append("running")
    return "running" in states


def hand_off_to_update_helper(argv):
    """Hand the room's process to the helper that outlives it (D-13).

    On Mac and Linux this execs the helper in place: same process id, same
    terminal, Ctrl+C still works, and the port is freed by the exec itself
    (RESEARCH Q3 probes). On Windows a directory cannot be renamed while a
    process runs inside it, so the helper starts detached with its working
    directory OUTSIDE the app folder and the server shuts itself down; the
    Windows branch is named honestly: it has not run on real Windows (the
    promise is tested on Macs).

    Called from a short one-shot timer AFTER the JSON answer has flushed,
    never from the route body. exec skips exit handlers, which is exactly
    why start_update_install refuses the tap while any job runs (RESEARCH
    Pitfall 7): nothing that reads her photographs may outlive the swap."""
    if update_job_running():
        # A job began after the route's own re-check answered the browser:
        # the exec would kill its worker thread mid-write. Abort the
        # hand-off entirely; the room keeps running, and the prepared tree
        # stays beside the app folder for the next tap.
        return
    if os.name != "nt":
        os.execv(argv[0], argv)
        return
    subprocess.Popen(
        argv, cwd=str(REPO_ROOT.parent),
        stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, close_fds=True,
        creationflags=(getattr(subprocess, "DETACHED_PROCESS", 0)
                       | getattr(subprocess, "CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP", 0)))
    if SERVER_INSTANCE is not None:
        # Never from the serve_forever thread; a worker thread is fine
        # (daemon_threads is True on StudyServer).
        threading.Thread(target=SERVER_INSTANCE.shutdown,
                         daemon=True).start()


def start_update_install():
    """The install route's body (26.9997 D-12..D-17): refuse, download,
    unpack, verify, then answer handing_off. Kept out of the handler so a
    suite drives it without a socket.

    The refusal order is fixed: no_stamp, no_consent, not_behind,
    job_running, dest_not_writable, download_failed, bad_zip,
    stamp_mismatch. Every refusal removes anything this tap created beside
    the app folder, so a failed tap leaves no half-download behind (D-17).
    The route never touches the library folder or anything of hers in the
    settings home (D-14): the download and the unpack land BESIDE the app
    folder, and the helper alone writes its result note.

    On success the answer carries the helper argv under "_helper_argv";
    the HANDLER pops it (it never reaches the browser) and starts the
    short one-shot timer that calls hand_off_to_update_helper, so the
    JSON answer flushes before the exec (RESEARCH Pattern 3)."""
    stamp = study_lib.read_release_stamp(REPO_ROOT)
    if not stamp:
        return {"ok": True, "refused": "no_stamp"}
    if study_lib.update_consent_state() != study_lib.UPDATE_CONSENT_STATE_CONSENTED:
        return {"ok": True, "refused": "no_consent"}
    latest = study_lib.read_latest_release_date()
    if not study_lib.compute_show_update_prompt(stamp, latest):
        return {"ok": True, "refused": "not_behind"}
    # exec skips exit handlers (RESEARCH Pitfall 7): a collect child must
    # never keep reading her photographs after the swap. The plan names
    # LIBRARIAN_JOB / ASK_JOB / IMPORT_JOB; the collect EXPORT phase and
    # the vision read spawn the same kind of child, so their job dicts
    # are refused too (same token, same one line on screen).
    if update_job_running():
        return {"ok": True, "refused": "job_running"}
    parent = REPO_ROOT.parent
    if not os.access(str(parent), os.W_OK):
        return {"ok": True, "refused": "dest_not_writable"}
    found = study_lib.fetch_latest_release()
    if found is None:
        return {"ok": True, "refused": "download_failed"}
    tag, asset_url, zipball_url = found
    source_url = asset_url or zipball_url
    if not source_url:
        return {"ok": True, "refused": "download_failed"}
    zip_path = parent / (REPO_ROOT.name + ".update-new-" + tag + ".zip")
    unpack_dir = parent / (REPO_ROOT.name + ".update-new-" + tag)

    def refuse_and_clean(token):
        try:
            if zip_path.is_file():
                zip_path.unlink()
        except OSError:
            pass
        shutil.rmtree(str(unpack_dir), ignore_errors=True)
        return {"ok": True, "refused": token}

    ok, _why = study_lib.download_release_zip(source_url, zip_path)
    if not ok:
        return refuse_and_clean("download_failed")
    try:
        helper = _update_room_module()
    except Exception:
        return refuse_and_clean("bad_zip")
    ok, _why = helper.unpack_release_zip(zip_path, unpack_dir)
    if not ok:
        return refuse_and_clean("bad_zip")
    root = helper.unpacked_tree_root(unpack_dir)
    if root is None:
        return refuse_and_clean("bad_zip")
    ok, _why = helper.verify_source_stamps(Path(root), expect=tag)
    if not ok:
        return refuse_and_clean("stamp_mismatch")
    # The download above may have blocked for up to
    # UPDATE_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT_S: a job started in another tab during that
    # window escaped the first check, and the exec would kill its worker
    # thread mid-write (an interrupted import discards the whole import).
    # Re-check before arming the hand-off; the refusal cleans up like any
    # other so a refused tap still leaves nothing behind (D-17).
    if update_job_running():
        return refuse_and_clean("job_running")
    argv = [sys.executable, str(REPO_ROOT / "tools" / "update_room.py"),
            "--swap-and-restart", "--new", str(root),
            "--dest", str(REPO_ROOT), "--expect", tag, "--restart"]
    return {"ok": True, "handing_off": True, "expect": tag,
            "_helper_argv": argv}


def record_base_consent_answer(address, answer):
    """Record her answer about the address CURRENTLY IN EFFECT, and return the
    resulting block. The route's whole body, kept out of the handler so a suite
    can drive it without a socket.

    ⛔ FAIL-CLOSED ON THE ADDRESS. The answer names the address the page was
    SHOWING, and it is recorded only while that is still the address in effect.
    A page painted before her settings file moved may not agree on her behalf
    to a host she is not looking at — which is D-09's own reason for the
    re-ask, applied to the round trip itself.

    ⛔ FAIL-CLOSED ON THE ANSWER TOO: only the yes token grants, because the
    safe direction for an answer nobody recognises is *not agreed*.
    `librarian_call.record_base_consent` holds the same rule; this one is not
    trusting that it does.

    ⛔ It never returns, echoes or logs a key. The only values it touches are
    an address and one of the two answer tokens."""
    try:
        routing = resolve_librarian_routing()
    except Exception:
        return base_consent_block()
    gap = base_consent_gap(routing)
    if gap is None:
        return base_consent_block()
    _provider, base, _state = gap
    if (librarian_call.normalise_base(address)
            != librarian_call.normalise_base(base)):
        return base_consent_block()
    librarian_call.record_base_consent(
        base,
        librarian_call.CONSENT_YES if answer == librarian_call.CONSENT_YES
        else librarian_call.CONSENT_NO)
    return base_consent_block()


def setup_check_key(provider, routing):
    """`--setup`'s one cloud request, with D-10's gate in front of it.

    Returns `(token, asked)`. `asked` is False when the check did not happen at
    all, so the caller reports a FACT rather than printing a failure sentence
    for a call nobody made.

    ✅ D-10'S CONSEQUENCE, TAKEN: the key check MAY follow the override,
    because the prompt precedes it. That is what makes a redirected address
    actually work instead of being quietly ignored — the shipped `check_key`
    defaulted to `DEFAULT_BASES` and a room that had redirected everything
    still had its key checked against the company it was avoiding.

    ⛔ AND A REFUSAL DOES NOT FALL BACK TO THE SHIPPED ADDRESS. D-15 rejected
    exactly that: the room would answer a refusal by doing the very thing that
    was refused, sending the key to the company redirecting the address may
    have been avoiding. With no consent the check simply does not happen."""
    if not base_agreed(provider, routing):
        return None, False
    base = (getattr(routing, "bases", None) or {}).get(provider)
    return librarian_call.check_key(provider, base=base), True


def _fill_trouble(fill, local_state):
    """The failure token that stops this fill from answering, or None.

    ⚠ THE CLOUD HALF READS PRESENCE AND A RECORDED REJECTION, AND NOTHING ELSE.
    `librarian_call.key_present` answers a boolean and the keys file's history
    carries one field, `rejected_ms`, written by one function, and only ever for
    a 401 or a 403. A 429, a 500, a 529 and no-network never reach that writer,
    because not one of them has looked at the credential — so there is nothing
    wider to read here even if a later reader wanted to. The history is read PER
    PROVIDER: a rejection recorded against one can never make the room doubt the
    other's key.

    ⚠ AND IT NEVER OPENS A CLOUD CONNECTION. Asking a company whether a key
    works costs money on every room open; `--setup` is where that one request is
    made, once, with the cost said out loud beforehand.

    `local_state` is a callable so her own machine is asked its one free
    question at most once per answer, however many tiers it happens to fill."""
    provider, _model = fill
    if provider in librarian_call.KEY_ENV_NAMES:
        if not librarian_call.key_present(provider):
            return "no_key"
        history = librarian_call.load_keys().get(provider) or {}
        if history.get("rejected_ms") is not None:
            return "bad_key"
        return None
    state = local_state()
    if state == librarian_call.PROBE_NOT_RUNNING:
        return "ollama_not_running"
    if state == librarian_call.PROBE_MODEL_MISSING:
        return "model_not_pulled"
    # ⚠ The SEARCH model is deliberately not consulted here (#35 section 4): a
    # room with the language model can still clean, label, name sections and
    # write a reflection, and those are real. Its absence is said out loud at
    # start-up instead of taking four working features away over a fifth.
    return None


def librarian_available(store):
    """The fail-open availability answer (SRM-11 criterion 1) — answered from
    the routing object, never by asking a binary on PATH whether it exists.

    Three questions, once each: does a tier hold a fill, does that fill's
    provider have something to authenticate with, and — when the fill is her own
    machine — is the model there. Returns the shipped shape unchanged,
    {available, why, auth, enabled, version_ok}, because the client,
    `handle_librarian_status` and `cleaning_available` all read it and not one of
    them is this plan's to change.

    ⚠ ONLY A RECORDED 401/403 MAY MOVE WHAT THE ROOM BELIEVES ABOUT A
    CREDENTIAL, AND THIS IS THE LAST PLACE THAT DISTINCTION CAN STILL BE LOST.
    A busy server, an unwell server, no network and a slow read have not looked
    at the credential; a room that treated any of them as a rejection would send
    her to find, delete and re-paste a perfectly good key. That does not merely
    tell her something untrue — it costs her work, and leaves her trusting the
    room less afterwards.

    ⚠ A ROOM WITH ONLY HER OWN MACHINE IN IT IS AVAILABLE (#28 section 1), and a
    missing SEARCH model is not a reason to say otherwise (#35 section 4).

    ⚠ NO KEY VALUE, AND NO FRAGMENT OF ONE, IS IN THIS RETURN AT ANY DEPTH.
    Presence is a boolean and the history is a timestamp; nothing here can hold
    a credential even by accident.

    FAIL-OPEN, ALWAYS. An unreadable keys file, a hand-edited settings file or a
    local question that raises answers unavailable with a plain sentence — never
    an exception into a handler, and never a room that will not open. A code
    fault is not one of the eleven things a call can come back saying, so it
    keeps the room's own error line rather than borrowing a failure's words."""
    meta = store.get("meta")
    meta = meta if isinstance(meta, dict) else {}
    # ⚠ ABSENT MEANS ON, through the shipped ONE reading of a switch of this
    # shape (`cleaning_flag_on`, 26.85-07) rather than a second copy of its
    # rule. ⚠ ONLY THE DEFAULT CHANGES HERE: a store that already carries her
    # choice keeps it, in either direction, and nothing in this function writes.
    enabled = cleaning_flag_on(meta, "librarian_enabled")
    try:
        routing = resolve_librarian_routing()
        # ⛔⛔ 26.99-09 (D-15 + D-16): THE SECOND, INDEPENDENT REASON TO ANSWER
        # UNAVAILABLE, and it is deliberately NOT a write into
        # `librarian_enabled` one line above. That key is MODEL-PROPOSABLE:
        # routing a consent record through it would let the librarian propose
        # flipping the answer to "may your key be sent to this host", which is
        # exactly what D-16 forbids. So the reason lives HERE, in the answer,
        # and the switch she set is returned untouched in either direction —
        # nothing in this function writes to the store at all.
        #
        # ⛔ AND IT SITS ABOVE THE PROBE ON PURPOSE. `local_state()` below
        # reaches her own machine at `_ollama_base(routing)`, which is an
        # address a settings file can redirect (D-19). Returning here is what
        # stops a room open from being a call to a host nobody agreed to; a
        # check placed after the loop would have already made it.
        #
        # ⚠ THE SHIPPED SHAPE IS UNCHANGED — {available, why, auth, enabled,
        # version_ok}, the five keys three consumers read. The reason rides
        # `why`, which is already a plain sentence, rather than a sixth field.
        #
        # ⚠ AND THE ROOM STILL OPENS (D-15, SRM-11). The base app has never
        # required a key, so everything that is not the librarian still works;
        # this is the librarian being off, which SRM-11 already requires the
        # app to survive, and not a fail-open librarian substitute (law 7).
        consent_gap = base_consent_gap(routing)
        if consent_gap is not None:
            return {"available": False,
                    "why": base_consent_why(consent_gap[2]),
                    "auth": LIBRARIAN_AUTH_TOKEN, "enabled": enabled,
                    "version_ok": False}
        asked = {}

        def local_state():
            if "state" not in asked:
                answer = librarian_call.probe_ollama(_ollama_base(routing))
                asked["state"] = (answer or {}).get("state")
            return asked["state"]

        reasons = []
        working = False
        for tier in librarian_call.TIERS:
            fill = routing.fills.get(tier)
            if not fill:
                # ⚠ NAMED, NEVER SUBSTITUTED (#27 section 5). The token names
                # the ACTUAL missing thing; no other tier's fill steps in.
                reasons.append((librarian_call.missing_fill_reason(tier), None))
                continue
            token = _fill_trouble(fill, local_state)
            if token is None:
                working = True
            else:
                reasons.append((token, fill[0]))
    except Exception:
        return {"available": False, "why": LIBRARIAN_ERROR_MSG,
                "auth": LIBRARIAN_AUTH_TOKEN, "enabled": enabled,
                "version_ok": False}

    if not working:
        token, provider = _first_trouble(reasons)
        return {"available": False,
                "why": failure_sentence(token, provider),
                "auth": LIBRARIAN_AUTH_TOKEN, "enabled": enabled,
                "version_ok": False}
    if not enabled:
        return {"available": False, "why": LIBRARIAN_OFF_MSG,
                "auth": LIBRARIAN_AUTH_TOKEN, "enabled": False,
                "version_ok": True}
    # `version_ok` no longer means a version — there is nothing to version. It
    # now reports whether SOMETHING can answer at all, which is the fact the
    # surfaces reading it were always using it for. Kept so no client has to
    # change; Plan 26.93-07 owns whether it goes.
    return {"available": True, "why": None, "auth": LIBRARIAN_AUTH_TOKEN,
            "enabled": True, "version_ok": True}


def _first_trouble(reasons):
    """(token, provider) for the trouble the room explains first, in
    `_UNAVAILABLE_ORDER`. Pure: reads a list of pairs."""
    for candidate in _UNAVAILABLE_ORDER:
        for token, provider in reasons:
            if token == candidate:
                return token, provider
    return reasons[0] if reasons else (None, None)


def _startup_local_lines(state, search_model):
    """Her own machine's THREE states, kept three, each with its own command.

    "Nothing is answering", "answering, but without the language model" and
    "answering, with it" are different facts with different things to do about
    them, and flattening any two sends her to the wrong command. The words for
    the two `ollama` commands are taken from the same map `--setup` reads, so
    each command is spelled exactly once in this file."""
    lines = []
    if state == librarian_call.PROBE_NOT_RUNNING:
        lines.append("  your own machine: nothing is answering.")
        lines.append("    to start it, run:   "
                     + _LOCAL_COMMANDS[librarian_call.PROBE_NOT_RUNNING])
    elif state == librarian_call.PROBE_MODEL_MISSING:
        lines.append("  your own machine: running, but the language model is "
                     "not there.")
        lines.append("    to fetch it, run:   "
                     + _LOCAL_COMMANDS[librarian_call.PROBE_MODEL_MISSING])
    else:
        lines.append("  your own machine: running, with the language model.")
    if not search_model:
        # ⚠ ITS OWN SENTENCE AND ITS OWN COMMAND — and NOT a reason to call the
        # librarian unavailable. Its symptom is a room that offers nothing,
        # which reads as the product being empty rather than as one model being
        # absent, so it has to be said rather than inferred.
        lines.append("    the search model is not there; the room cannot "
                     "search your own things until it is.")
        lines.append("    to fetch it, run:   " + _SEARCH_MODEL_COMMAND)
    return lines


def startup_librarian_check(routing, probe=librarian_call.probe_ollama):
    """Who is answering, tier by tier, BEFORE anything is asked of anyone.

    Returns a list of plain lines and puts nothing on the screen itself;
    `main()` does that. The split is what lets a suite assert the words without
    capturing stdout, and it is why this function opens no file, takes no lock
    and touches no store.

    ⚠ WHAT IT MAY COST IS WHAT DECIDES WHAT IT MAY DO. Her own machine is asked
    ONE free question — the local tag list, offline, milliseconds — so the room
    can say which of three states it is in. A cloud provider cannot be asked
    anything at all without paying for it, on every single room open, so those
    tiers are INSPECTED and never called: a key is reported present or absent,
    and nothing more. ⚠ THE ROOM NEVER SAYS A KEY WORKS HERE. `--setup` is the
    one place a credential is checked, once, with the cost said out loud first.
    Pretending the room can say the same thing about all three providers is
    exactly how the over-claim bug comes back wearing a new costume.

    ⚠ NO KEY VALUE, NO FRAGMENT OF ONE, AT ANY LENGTH, reaches a line returned
    from here. One key per provider means a masked fragment would disambiguate
    nothing while creating something worth screenshotting.

    ⚠ A MISSING FILL IS NAMED, NEVER SUBSTITUTED (#27 section 5). A tier with
    nothing in it says so and says what that costs; no other tier's fill is
    reported in its place and nothing quietly steps in.

    It states a fact and does not ask for anything: it never suggests she ought
    to add a key, and it never references how long it has been since the room was
    opened (law 3). With no cloud key anywhere the documented order lands on her
    own machine, and that is a complete room (#28 section 1) — not an apology.

    `probe` is a parameter with a default so a suite can hand in a fake, and this
    check never needs a live local server to be exercised."""
    lines = ["The librarian"]
    asked = {}

    # ⛔⛔ 26.99-09 (D-19, the WIDE reading of D-10): THE PROBE BELOW IS THE
    # ROOM'S FIRST NETWORK CALL OF EVERY SESSION, AND ITS ADDRESS IS
    # REDIRECTABLE. `librarian_call.resolve_routing`'s settings `bases` loop
    # has always accepted an override for all three providers — its own comment
    # concedes nobody should build on that — so a hand-edited settings file has
    # been pointing this call at an arbitrary host with nothing asked. No
    # credential rides it, so it is not a key leak; D-10's words are "no call
    # of ANY kind", and this is a call of a kind. ⛔ The narrow reading was
    # refused in writing; the finding is routed to #12.
    #
    # ⚠ COMPUTED ONCE, HERE, AND READ ONCE, BELOW — `local_answer` has exactly
    # one call site, so one guard covers the whole function and there is no
    # second place for the two to drift apart.
    #
    # ⚠⚠ A ROOM WITH NO OVERRIDE TAKES A BYTE-IDENTICAL PATH.
    # `base_is_shipped_default` is pure and answers first, so this line opens
    # no file, asks nobody and cannot raise for the overwhelming majority who
    # redirected nothing — which is the whole reason a gate is allowed on the
    # path every user hits at all.
    probe_gated = librarian_call.base_needs_consent(_ollama_base(routing),
                                                    "ollama")

    def local_answer():
        # AT MOST ONE local question per start-up, however many tiers her own
        # machine happens to fill.
        if "answer" not in asked:
            answer = probe(_ollama_base(routing))
            asked["answer"] = answer if isinstance(answer, dict) else {}
        return asked["answer"]

    # ⚠⚠ ONE LINE PER TIER NAMING A PROVIDER AND A MODEL, AND THAT IS ONLY
    # POSSIBLE BECAUSE OF D-05. A fill names a provider AND a model, so exactly
    # one provider serves a tier at any moment and there is no precedence race
    # left to resolve. Under a "try one, then fall back to the other" design
    # there would be nothing truthful to say before the first call — the room
    # would only learn who answered by asking. That is why no fallback exists
    # anywhere in `librarian_call`: nothing there tries a second provider after
    # a first one failed, and this line is what that buys.
    cloud_seen = []
    local_seen = False
    for tier in librarian_call.TIERS:
        label = _TIER_WORDS.get(tier, tier) + ":"
        fill = routing.fills.get(tier)
        if not fill:
            lines.append("  %-20s nothing fills this." % label)
            lines.append("    the jobs pinned to it will refuse until "
                         "something does. Nothing answers in its place.")
            continue
        provider, model = fill
        source = _PROVENANCE_WORDS.get(routing.provenance.get(tier), "")
        lines.append("  %-20s %s · %s   (%s)" % (label, provider, model,
                                                 source))
        if provider in librarian_call.KEY_ENV_NAMES:
            if provider not in cloud_seen:
                cloud_seen.append(provider)
        else:
            local_seen = True

    # ---- 26.94-03 (D-03, SRM-11): the one line that is NOT a tier ---------
    #
    # ⚠ WHY THIS SITS OUTSIDE THE LOOP, AND WHY `on-device` IS NOT A `TIERS`
    # MEMBER. Reading photographs is served by a framework on this machine,
    # not by a language model: it has no provider, no model, no key and no
    # price. A row for it in `librarian_call.TIERS` would therefore be NULL in
    # every column its consumers read — `routing.fills`, `routing.provenance`
    # and the `JOBS` map — and the tier table's whole promise is that each
    # line names a provider AND a model. A row that could name neither would
    # make the table say something untrue about itself. So the report gains a
    # NAMED LINE after the tier lines instead of a fourth tuple member; the
    # same hand-off is answered, in the same words, at `librarian_call.py`'s
    # `TIERS` (26.94-02). The accepted debt is exactly this special case, and
    # what would overturn it is a SECOND non-LLM tier.
    #
    # ⚠ AND WHY THIS IS THE SEARCH MODEL'S SHAPE AND NOT `nothing fills this.`
    # The two shapes answer two different absences. `nothing fills this.` is
    # for a tier whose fill is a MODEL: naming what it costs is the whole
    # duty, and a command there would be the room asking her for a key —
    # exactly what this function's docstring forbids. The line below names an
    # OS COMPONENT that ships free with macOS, and its fix is one documented
    # invocation with nothing to buy, nothing to sign in to and nobody to
    # tell. That is why the search model's two-line fact-then-command form —
    # eight lines above, in `_startup_local_lines` — is the honest precedent
    # here, and it is copied rather than re-invented.
    #
    # ⚠ IT STATES A FACT AND ASKS FOR NOTHING. No "you should", no "we
    # recommend", no cost, no provider, no key. Every other feature works; the
    # room simply cannot read pictures, and it says so rather than failing at
    # the moment she asks. The wording is the owner's, approved 2026-08-13.
    #
    # The probe asks the FILESYSTEM only and spawns nothing (26.94-02), so
    # this costs the front door no process and can never open the installer's
    # dialog. It is also pure with respect to time: two calls with the same
    # routing produce byte-identical text.
    if _swiftc_path() is None:
        lines.append("  reading photographs:  the Command Line Tools are "
                     "not there; the room cannot read your photographs "
                     "until they are.")
        lines.append("    to fetch them, run:   " + _CLT_INSTALL_COMMAND)

    for provider in cloud_seen:
        # PRESENCE, AND NOTHING MORE. No connection is opened to find out any
        # more than this, and there is nothing more that could be said for free.
        lines.append("  %s: %s" % (
            provider,
            "a key is present." if librarian_call.key_present(provider)
            else "no key here."))
    if local_seen and not probe_gated:
        answer = local_answer()
        lines.extend(_startup_local_lines(answer.get("state"),
                                          bool(answer.get("search_model"))))
    # ⛔⛔ AND WHEN IT IS GATED, NOTHING IS SAID ABOUT HER OWN MACHINE AT ALL.
    # ⚠ THE TEMPTING WRONG MOVE IS TO CALL `_startup_local_lines(None, False)`
    # AND LET IT SPEAK: it would print "nothing is answering" and "the search
    # model is not there", and BOTH would be claims about a machine this room
    # has just been told it may not ask. A report that answers a question it
    # was not permitted to put is worse than a report that is short.
    #
    # ⛔ AND NO NEW SENTENCE IS INVENTED HERE EITHER (D-14). Every sentence a
    # person reads in this room is the owner's, taken as one pass, and no
    # sentence was asked for "the start-up report is quiet because you have not
    # answered yet". A slot with no words renders NOTHING — the shipped posture
    # of every other surface in this phase. Her §S-08 question is waiting in
    # `--setup` and in Manage, which are the two sites the copy record names.
    #
    # ⚠ IT NEVER RAISES AND NEVER BLOCKS START-UP. This is a shorter report,
    # not a failure: the tier lines above are unchanged, the room opens, and
    # everything that is not the librarian still works (D-15, SRM-11).
    return lines


def validate_librarian_enabled(data):
    """The librarian_enabled meta flag is a bool, exactly (the
    validate_consolidation register style). Fail-closed: anything else is
    refused at the write.

    ⚠ 26.93-08 changed the DEFAULT this key reads with, and nothing else: an
    absent flag now means ON (`librarian_available` reads it through
    `cleaning_flag_on`, 26.85-07's one reading of a switch of this shape). A
    stored value is untouched in either direction, and the room is AI-native —
    law 7 as amended — so a flag she has to go hunting for in Manage is a hand
    that is not offered. This validator's job is unchanged: only a real bool
    may ever be written.
    Returns a plain-words error string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "librarian_enabled" in data:
        v = data["librarian_enabled"]
        if not isinstance(v, bool):
            return "librarian_enabled must be true or false."
    return None


def validate_fenced_roster(data):
    """The fenced_roster meta key is a LIST OF STRINGS, exactly (26.4-01,
    D-05). Fail-closed: the roster drives the born-flag that fences whole
    folders from the librarian, so a non-list or a non-string entry must be
    refused at the write — a malformed roster that silently matched nothing
    would fail-OPEN the fence (the validate_filters posture). An absent key
    means the shipped default roster (study_lib.DEFAULT_FENCED_ROSTER).
    Returns a plain-words error string, or None when acceptable.

    26.87-07 (SE-9, T-26.87-02) — WHY THIS VALIDATOR IS NOT ENOUGH, and why
    the config ask must never POST a roster diff here: this checks a list of
    strings and NOTHING more, and handle_meta then MERGES it. That merge
    SKIPS study_lib.add_roster_folder's retroactive trigger=True stamping —
    the stamping that closed the 26.4-01 hole where a folder added to the
    roster left its already-imported items still surfacing, a fence hole at
    exactly the place the user asked for privacy. So a roster diff through
    this door would pass every validator here and silently reopen it. The
    roster route is the only legal door; the client's confirm path dispatches
    there and refuses a mixed roster+meta list outright."""
    if "fenced_roster" in data:
        v = data["fenced_roster"]
        if not isinstance(v, list) \
                or not all(isinstance(x, str) for x in v):
            return "fenced_roster must be a list of folder names."
    return None


def validate_sync_comments(data):
    """The sync_comments_enabled meta flag is a bool, exactly (26.4-01,
    D-11). Fail-closed: this switch alone gates the ONE new writer into the
    user's vault, so a non-bool is refused at the write; an absent flag means
    OFF (nothing in the vault is ever touched). Returns a plain-words error
    string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "sync_comments_enabled" in data:
        v = data["sync_comments_enabled"]
        if not isinstance(v, bool):
            return "sync_comments_enabled must be true or false."
    return None


def validate_last_visit_ms(data):
    """The last_visit_ms meta key is a whole number OR null (26.4-01, D-08).
    Silent machinery: it is NEVER rendered or returned to a surface (law 3),
    but it must still be shape-checked fail-closed at the write — a bad value
    could skew the since-last-visit 'now' threshold. null = first visit.
    Returns a plain-words error string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "last_visit_ms" in data:
        v = data["last_visit_ms"]
        if v is not None and (isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, int)):
            return "last_visit_ms must be a whole number or null."
    return None


def validate_last_reflection_ms(data):
    """The last_reflection_ms meta key is a whole number OR null (26.7-02,
    D-11). Silent machinery: it is NEVER rendered or returned to a surface
    (law 3), but it must still be shape-checked fail-closed at the write —
    a bad value could skew the session pool's strict-> boundary. null =
    first session (the whole allowed archive is the pool). READ at pool
    build; stamped at session COMPLETION only, never by session start, a
    progress poll, or a nothing-new answer. Returns a plain-words error
    string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "last_reflection_ms" in data:
        v = data["last_reflection_ms"]
        if v is not None and (isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, int)):
            return "last_reflection_ms must be a whole number or null."
    return None


def validate_reflection_writeback(data):
    """The reflection_writeback_enabled meta flag is a bool, exactly
    (26.7-04, D-06 — validate_sync_comments' twin). Fail-closed: this
    switch alone gates the SECOND disclosed writer into the user's vault
    (new-file-only, into Claude's observation/Journal analysis/), so a
    non-bool is refused at the write; an absent flag means OFF (a saved
    book changes zero vault bytes — ever). Returns a plain-words error
    string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "reflection_writeback_enabled" in data:
        v = data["reflection_writeback_enabled"]
        if not isinstance(v, bool):
            return "reflection_writeback_enabled must be true or false."
    return None


def validate_librarian_name(data):
    """The librarian_name meta key is a SHORT string (<= 40 chars) OR null
    (26.6-02, SRM-06, D-02). Fail-closed (the validate_librarian_enabled
    posture): the candle's chosen name persists here, so a non-string or an
    over-long name is refused at the write; null is the D-02 skip path (the
    candle keeps its default unnamed presence), and an absent key leaves the
    prior name untouched (shallow-merge discipline). The 40-char soft cap
    matches the UI-SPEC long-name resolution (E2 overflow). The name is
    never echoed server-side — the escape seam renders it client-side (Plan
    03). Returns a plain-words error string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "librarian_name" in data:
        v = data["librarian_name"]
        if v is not None and (not isinstance(v, str) or len(v) > 40):
            return "that name is a little long. a short one, please."
    return None


def validate_onboarding_complete(data):
    """The onboarding_complete meta flag is a bool, exactly (26.6-02,
    SRM-06). Fail-closed: the returning-path gate consults this flag
    alongside items>=1, so a non-bool is refused at the write; an absent
    flag means not-yet-complete (the safe default). Returns a plain-words
    error string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "onboarding_complete" in data:
        v = data["onboarding_complete"]
        if not isinstance(v, bool):
            return "onboarding_complete must be true or false."
    return None


def validate_connected_sources(data):
    """The connected_sources meta key is a LIST of known adapter source
    names, exactly (26.65-05, ADP-03). Fail-closed: Manage's disconnect is
    the one client write through here — it removes a connection and nothing
    else, so a non-list, an unknown name, or a duplicate is refused at the
    write. The shallow merge writes meta alone: items.json is never touched
    by a disconnect, so everything already brought in stays in the room
    (law 3, D-02). Returns a plain-words error string, or None when
    acceptable."""
    if "connected_sources" in data:
        v = data["connected_sources"]
        if not isinstance(v, list) \
                or not all(isinstance(x, str) and x in ADAPTER_SOURCES
                           for x in v) \
                or len(v) != len(set(v)):
            return "connected_sources must list known app sources only."
    return None


def validate_voice_model(data):
    """The voice_model meta key is a MEMBER of VOICE_MODELS, exactly
    (26.87-09, D-18/D-19, T-26.87-03) — validate_connected_sources'
    CLOSED-MEMBERSHIP register, never validate_librarian_enabled's boolean
    one: this is a closed string enum, and a boolean check is the wrong
    shape for it entirely.

    WHY FAIL-CLOSED MATTERS FOR *THIS* KEY SPECIFICALLY, and it is not the
    generic reason: every other meta key is read by our own code. This one
    is handed to a SUBPROCESS as the CLI's --model argument. So an unknown
    alias — a pinned model id, a fourth alias the vendor ships, a typo, an
    arbitrary string — is refused HERE, at the write, and never stored. The
    stored value can then never be the thing that puts an arbitrary string
    on the argv; _allowed_model is the second line at read time, for a
    store hand-edited outside the app (the cleaning_flag_on posture).

    The allow-list's narrowing versus the CLI's own wider alias set is
    DELIBERATE (D-31.5) — see the comment on VOICE_MODELS. An ABSENT key
    means LIBRARIAN_VOICE_MODEL_DEFAULT: no migration, no schema-version
    bump. Returns a plain-words error string naming the legal options, or
    None when acceptable."""
    if "voice_model" in data:
        v = data["voice_model"]
        if not isinstance(v, str) or v not in VOICE_MODELS:
            return ("the voice can be " + ", ".join(VOICE_MODELS[:-1]) +
                    " or " + VOICE_MODELS[-1] + ", nothing else.")
    return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 26.95-34 (D-16): the blessing pass's size, and its two bounds.
#
# Tunable data beside the function that enforces it, with the reason attached
# rather than left in a plan nobody re-reads. A batch size decides HOW MUCH OF
# HER ARCHIVE IS PUT IN FRONT OF HER AT ONCE, so it gets a floor and a ceiling
# for two different reasons:
#
#   * the FLOOR keeps a pass from being pointless. Zero would present nothing
#     at all, and a pass that presents nothing is not a gentler pass — it is a
#     broken one. One is the smallest pass that is still a pass, and it is
#     deliberately reachable: the whole reason this key exists is that she
#     asked for SMALLER batches (26.87's D-35.4 records it as her own example
#     ask), so a floor that refused two would answer a gentle ask with a
#     refusal.
#   * the CEILING keeps a pass from becoming a wall. It bounds how many items
#     one pass can allocate and render, so neither a stored value nor a
#     model-proposed one can turn a gentle pass into a whole-archive render
#     (T-26.95-25). Fifty is five times the shipped default — long enough for
#     the deliberate grind in Manage to be worth sitting down for — and far
#     below the size of the pool it draws from.
#
# ⚠ BOTH NUMBERS ARE THE OWNER'S TO SET. The plan named the two REASONS and
# left the values open; these are this commit's reading of those reasons.
# Changing either is a one-line edit that nothing else depends on numerically:
# the refusal sentence below is BUILT from these constants, so a change can
# never leave the room naming a range it no longer enforces.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLESSING_BATCH_MIN = 1
BLESSING_BATCH_MAX = 50

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 26.95-34 Task 2 (D-16): THE SHIPPED DEFAULT, MIRRORED HERE SO THE ASK CAN
# ANSWER "how many is it right now?" TRUTHFULLY.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS A SECOND HOME FOR A NUMBER THAT LIVES IN ANOTHER LANGUAGE, and it
# is written that way on purpose rather than by accident. The number the key
# actually drives is core.js's `DEFAULTS.BLESSING_COUNT` — startBlessing omits
# the opt when the key is absent, so core's own default governs the pass. The
# server needs the same number to report an ABSENT key's effective value into
# the ask document, and it cannot read a .js file at import.
#
# So the two are PINNED AGAINST EACH OTHER BY VALUE by
# tests/test_bless_batch.cjs, the way this project already pins
# WALK_FIRST_WINDOW_MS against REFLECTION_FIRST_WINDOW_MS and
# OFFER_MOMENT_CAP against OFFER_SLOTS_PER_PAGE: neither is asserted against a
# literal, each is asserted against the OTHER, so drift in either direction
# fails the suite instead of quietly making the room describe a pass it does
# not run.
#
# ⚠ app.js's `BLESS_BATCH = 10` is a THIRD "about ten" and it is deliberately
# NOT in this pin. It is the desk/pile sitting size, it is not driven by this
# key (26.95-34's two-constants ruling, with the measurement in the plan), and
# tying it in here would forbid a divergence the ruling explicitly permits. It
# keeps its own by-value pin in the same suite.
#
# ⚠ AND IT MUST BE A LEGAL VALUE. A default outside BLESSING_BATCH_MIN..MAX
# would make the ask document report a `now` that validate_blessing_batch_size
# itself would refuse — the room describing a state it would not accept. Pinned
# as an invariant, not left to reading.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLESSING_BATCH_DEFAULT = 10


def validate_blessing_batch_size(data):
    """The blessing_batch_size meta key is a WHOLE NUMBER inside
    BLESSING_BATCH_MIN..BLESSING_BATCH_MAX, exactly (26.95-34, D-16,
    T-26.95-23).

    ⚠ THIS IS validate_voice_model's POSTURE WITH A RANGE TEST, AND IT IS NOT
    validate_voice_model's SHAPE. That one is closed-STRING membership against
    a public constant; this one is an integer with two bounds. What carries
    over is the posture — fail-closed, refused AT THE WRITE and never stored,
    a plain-words error naming the legal values — and saying so out loud is
    the point: copying the membership shape onto a number would be the wrong
    register, and pretending the two are identical is exactly how a wrong
    register gets copied.

    ⚠ A BOOL IS REFUSED EXPLICITLY, AND IT HAS TO BE: `isinstance(True, int)`
    is True in Python, so a bare integer check would quietly store `True` as a
    batch size of one. The guard is validate_last_visit_ms' shipped one —
    `isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, int)` — with bounds added. The
    same clause refuses a float (2.0 is not an int) and a numeric STRING:
    coercing "10" here would put a coercion inside the fence, and the write
    path is the wrong place to be generous.

    WHY FAIL-CLOSED MATTERS FOR *THIS* KEY: the stored value decides how much
    of her archive one pass sets in front of her at once, and an unbounded one
    turns a gentle pass into a wall — which is the whole reason the ceiling
    exists. This validator is the FIRST line and the only one that can refuse;
    whatever reads the key is the SECOND, and an out-of-range value smuggled
    past here by a hand edit must read as ABSENT wherever it is read (the
    cleaning_flag_on posture for a store edited outside the app).

    An ABSENT key means the shipped default batch size: no migration, no
    schema-version bump. ⚠ NULL IS NOT ACCEPTED, deliberately — `null` is a
    non-integer like any other, "back to the default" is not a value this key
    has, and handle_meta's shallow merge cannot delete a key anyway, so a
    reset is simply writing the default number. Returns a plain-words error
    string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "blessing_batch_size" in data:
        v = data["blessing_batch_size"]
        if isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, int) \
                or v < BLESSING_BATCH_MIN or v > BLESSING_BATCH_MAX:
            return ("blessing_batch_size must be a whole number from " +
                    str(BLESSING_BATCH_MIN) + " to " +
                    str(BLESSING_BATCH_MAX) + ".")
    return None


def validate_display_fence_open(data):
    """The display_fence_open ask key is a bool, exactly (the
    validate_librarian_enabled register style). Fail-closed: anything else
    is refused and the proposed change is dropped (#105, 2026-08-25).

    ⚠ THIS VALIDATOR GUARDS A KEY THE SERVER NEVER STORES. The value lives
    in the browser's own storage (the reflection display fence, 26.4-10 —
    "this setting stays on this device"), so there is no write path here to
    protect; what this bounds is the ASK CHANNEL. validate_config_proposal
    consults it before a change may reach the card, and `_validate is None`
    is a DROP — without this entry the ask could name the key and never
    reach it, silently, which is the exact failure the voice_model and
    blessing_batch_size notes in _CONFIG_KEY_VALIDATORS both record.

    ⚠ IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN META_KEYS, so /api/meta refuses the key as
    unknown before any validator runs — this function must never be read as
    evidence the server can apply it. The sink is the client's own storage,
    and her tap in the card is the only writer, there.

    Returns a plain-words error string, or None when acceptable."""
    if "display_fence_open" in data and \
            not isinstance(data["display_fence_open"], bool):
        return "display_fence_open must be true or false."
    return None


def cleaning_flag_on(meta, key):
    """THE ONE reading of a tidy-up switch (26.85-07, owner's UAT call
    2026-07-30): ABSENT means ON.

    The tidy-up used to be `meta.get(key) is True` — absent = off — and the
    owner changed that live: a hand you have to go hunting for in Manage is
    a hand that is not offered. The feature is there on arrival instead of
    found. What protects her was never that the app COULD not write; it is
    that the app never writes unasked and everything it writes comes back
    (LIBRARIAN.md says exactly that now).

    Still fail-CLOSED on anything that is not a real yes: only a stored
    `true` or a missing key reads as on, so an explicit `false` is off and
    a hand-edited `"yes"` / `null` / `1` is ALSO off. The /api/meta
    validators keep refusing non-bools at the write; this is the second
    line for a store edited outside the app. Pure: reads a dict."""
    return (meta or {}).get(key, True) is True


def validate_cleaning_enabled(data):
    """The cleaning_enabled meta flag is a bool, exactly (26.85-04, D-06 —
    validate_librarian_enabled's twin). Fail-closed: this switch alone
    decides whether the tidy-up tier exists at all, so a non-bool is
    refused at the write. An ABSENT flag means ON (26.85-07 — see
    cleaning_flag_on); an explicit `false` is the byte-identical no-AI room
    (the clean routes then do zero work and answer available=false, never
    an error). Returns a plain-words error string, or None when
    acceptable."""
    if "cleaning_enabled" in data:
        v = data["cleaning_enabled"]
        if not isinstance(v, bool):
            return "cleaning_enabled must be true or false."
    return None


# ⛔ `validate_cleaning_writeback_enabled` STOOD HERE AND IS DELETED (owner
# 2026-08-17). It refused non-bools for the tidy-up's SECOND switch, the
# source-write consent (26.85-04, D-03). #95 deleted the labelling apply that
# was the only route consulting that switch; the readability write has never
# consulted it. The key is off CONFIGURABLE_KEYS now, so /api/meta refuses it
# by whitelist before any validator would run — which is why this deletion
# removes a check without opening a hole. See the meta-key comment above for
# why the two-permission split was not rebuilt onto the readability pass.


def cleaning_available(store):
    """The tidy-up tier's fail-open availability answer (26.85-04, D-06):
    librarian_available AND the cleaning_enabled meta flag true. Returns
    {available, why, auth, enabled} — unavailable carries a plain-words
    `why` and is NEVER an error state in the room.

    The order mirrors librarian_available exactly: not-set-up wins over
    off, because "sign in to Claude Code" is the more useful sentence when
    both are true. `enabled` reports the flag itself, so a surface can say
    "off" rather than "missing".

    ⛔ A `writeback` FIELD IS GONE FROM THIS ANSWER (owner 2026-08-17). It
    reported the second switch for disclosure, and a status field that
    reports a permission nothing enforces is a false sentence in the one
    place a surface would go to ask. The flag reads through
    cleaning_flag_on, so ABSENT is ON (26.85-07): the tidy-up is offered on
    arrival, and turning it off is what takes it away."""
    meta = store.get("meta") or {}
    enabled = cleaning_flag_on(meta, "cleaning_enabled")
    avail = librarian_available(store)
    if not avail["available"]:
        return {"available": False, "why": avail["why"],
                "auth": avail["auth"], "enabled": enabled}
    if not enabled:
        return {"available": False, "why": CLEAN_OFF_MSG,
                "auth": avail["auth"], "enabled": False}
    return {"available": True, "why": None, "auth": avail["auth"],
            "enabled": True}


def librarian_payload_text(store, scope, consent=False, store_dir=None):
    """Serialize the fence builder's return for the one call function.

    WIRING CONTRACT (checker W3, pinned statically in test_no_push.cjs
    26-01): the variable below is named `payload` and is assigned ONLY
    from study_lib.build_librarian_payload — renaming either side of that
    line consciously edits the pin. Store-derived bytes reach a model
    through this function and nowhere else. The only sanctioned
    NON-builder additions (AI-SPEC checker W2 — exactly FIVE,
    consciously extended 2->4 by 26.7-01 and 4->5 by 26.87-10, composed
    into the caller's document) are: (1) the notebook digest (aggregate
    counts and shelf words only — never item ids, titles, or bodies;
    26-03), (2) the dismissed-topics list (the user's own phrasings,
    user-authored by definition; 26-03), (3) the model's OWN prior
    structured output, re-entrant — the prior draft (26.7) and, WIDENED by
    26.87-10, the TITLES and derived opening-SHAPE TOKENS of prior drafts
    (the variation memory: still the model's own prior output handed back,
    which is why this is a widening of an existing class rather than a new
    one; the opening rides as a five-token label, never as a sentence),
    (4) her chat messages (user-authored by definition; 26.7), and (5) NEW
    with 26.87-10 — the IDENTITY ANCHORS: store-derived theme and folder
    tokens plus her own verbatim comment phrases. That fifth one is a
    genuinely new class and its fence argument belongs here: the anchors
    are built through the SAME shipped _librarian_fenced predicate this
    builder uses (called, never copied), so no never-show, retired,
    trigger-marked or filtered item can contribute a single token; tokens
    come from tags, the folder facet and the topic slug and NEVER from a
    title or a body; and the phrases are her own words, which are already
    a sanctioned class. All five still flow through the one call function's
    recorded request body, so every fence scan sees them too."""
    payload = study_lib.build_librarian_payload(
        store, scope, consent=consent, store_dir=store_dir)
    return json.dumps(payload, ensure_ascii=False)


